<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Tlatelolco</h1><p class="st-meta">1337 CE – 1473 CE (approx.) · capital Tlatelolco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The twin city of Tenochtitlan on an island in Lake Texcoco, home to the greatest market of the Americas, independent until 1473.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tlatelolco was founded in 1337, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> chronicles, by a group that split from the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> and settled on an adjacent island. Its first ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuacuauhpitzahuac">Cuacuauhpitzahuac</a>, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tezozomoc">Tezozomoc</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tepanec-empire">Tepanec Empire</a>, under whose protection it grew.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tlatelolco had its own tlatoani and a great temple. Its market, described by Cort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Díaz del Castillo</a>, reportedly drew some 60,000 people a day, trading food, cloth, obsidian, feathers and slaves. Its merchants (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pochteca">pochteca</a>) carried trade across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> were close but competitive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1473 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> defeated Tlatelolco and killed its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moquihuix">Moquihuix</a>, and the city was annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> under a military governor. In 1521 it saw the last Aztec stand against Cortés. Its site is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaza-de-las-tres-culturas">Plaza of the Three Cultures</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1337 CE – 1473 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.31%;width:2.71%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1337 CE – 1473 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tlatelolco</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cuacuauhpitzahuac, Moquihuix</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nahuatl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tlatelolco altepetl, Mexico-Tlatelolco</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1405&amp;focus=tlatelolco" aria-label="Tlatelolco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2002 1457 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tlatelolco lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2322 1713L2320 1712M2322 1713L2322 1714"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2320 1712h0M2322 1714h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2322 1713h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2322 1713h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tlatelolco. <a href="/atlas?year=1405&amp;focus=tlatelolco">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
